+Answer: ttl is in seconds
+
+# 2026-11-11 quickcache `ttl` unit
+
+**Question.** src/cache.js creates `new QuickCache({ ttl: 5000, max: 500 })`, meaning product entries should expire after 5 seconds. Is that what happens with the quickcache version installed in this repo?
+
+**Answer.** No. The installed quickcache (2.0.1) reads `ttl` as **seconds**, so `ttl: 5000` keeps product entries for 5000 s (about 83 minutes), not 5 s. To get a 5-second expiry with this version, use `ttl: 5`.
+
+**Findings.**
+- *Read:* package.json pulls quickcache from `file:vendor/quickcache`. vendor/quickcache/package.json says version `2.0.1`.
+- *Read:* vendor/quickcache/index.js (2.0.1), in the constructor: `this.ttlMs = ttl * 1000;`. `set()` then stores `expires: Date.now() + this.ttlMs`. So `ttl` is in seconds.
+- *Measured:* running `new QuickCache({ ttl: 5000 })` from vendor/quickcache under Node on 2026-11-11 gave `ttlMs = 5000000`. A `set()` entry's expiry was 5000 s in the future.
+- *Read:* the vendored README (vendor/quickcache/README.md, 2.0.1) says only "`ttl` (time to live; `0` disables expiry)" and gives no unit.
+- *Read:* the saved docs at docs/vendor/quickcache-docs.html are for **1.4.2** (saved 2024-05-02 from https://quickcache.example/docs). They say `ttl` is "in milliseconds". They do not describe the installed 2.x version. That mismatch is probably where the `5000` came from.
+- *Inferred:* the unit changed from milliseconds to seconds between 1.4.x and 2.0.x. No changelog is vendored to confirm when.
+
+**Sources.**
+- vendor/quickcache/index.js, quickcache 2.0.1 (commit 2cfd294), read 2026-11-11.
+- vendor/quickcache/package.json and README.md, quickcache 2.0.1, read 2026-11-11.
+- docs/vendor/quickcache-docs.html, quickcache 1.4.2 docs (saved 2024-05-02), read 2026-11-11.
+- src/cache.js and package.json (commit 2cfd294), read 2026-11-11.
+
+**Open questions.**
+- No 2.x changelog or upstream 2.x docs were available to confirm the release where the unit changed (https://quickcache.example/docs was not fetched).
+- `node_modules` is not installed in this checkout. This note assumes that `npm install` would link the vendored 2.0.1 package, as package.json says.
